ready: 1
kind: rule
id: retention.rule.purge-scope
display_id: RULE-RETENTION-PURGE-SCOPE
module: retention
title: What a purge removes
summary: A purge removes the memory and everything that holds its content, offers the owner the memories that depend on it, and queues the cloud deletion.
table:
  columns: [what, removed, how]
  rows:
    - [The memory row and every version of it, always, deleted]
    - [Its provenance links in both directions, always, deleted]
    - [Its full-text row and vector, always, "deleted before the purge returns"]
    - [Raw captures and transcript events cited only by it, always, deleted]
    - [Suggestions that cite it or were promoted into it, always, deleted]
    - [Its appraisal and usage rows, always, deleted]
    - [Recall log rows for it, always, deleted]
    - [Pending sync changes that carry its content, always, "payload removed and replaced by one deletion change"]
    - [Its line in exported files, always, export files rewritten without it]
    - [Its change-ledger rows, kept, "kept without content or name, so the owner can see something was purged"]
    - [Memories derived from it or merged into it, owner choice, "listed in the preview as optional additions"]
    - [Superseded predecessors, always for an owner purge, "followed recursively"]
constraints:
  - text: Every database connection uses secure delete, the full-text index is optimized and the write-ahead log is truncated before the purge returns.
    hard: true
  - text: The runtimes' own transcripts are outside Kernle's files, and the preview says so.
    hard: true
rationale: >-
  "Deleted" must mean gone from Kernle's files, so every copy of the content is
  named here, including the ones that are easy to miss: old versions, the
  search index, sync payloads and suggestion text. The ledger keeps a
  content-free row so the owner can still see that something was deleted and
  when. Dependent memories are offered rather than taken, because the owner did
  not ask for them and a lesson may stand on other evidence too.
